COACHING

Developing OCR Coaches in Ireland

The Obstacle Course Racing Association of Ireland (OCRA ÉIREANN) is developing a structured national coaching pathway for obstacle sport in Ireland.

Good coaching is fundamental to the long-term development of OCR.

Whether someone is introducing beginners to their first obstacles, supporting a local club or preparing athletes for international competition, coaches have an important responsibility for athlete development, safety and the quality of the sporting experience.

The OCRA Coaching Programme provides a pathway for coaches to develop from foundational education through participation, performance and high-performance coaching.

The OCRA Coaching Pathway

The coaching pathway consists of seven progressive levels:

1. OCR Coach — Foundations
2. OCR Coach — Participation
3. OCR Coach — Performance
4. OCR Coach — National
5. OCR Coach — International
6. OCR Coach — Senior International
7. OCR Coach — Master Coach Trainer

Coaches progress through the pathway as their knowledge, practical experience and responsibilities develop.

OCR Coach — Foundations

Foundations is the entry point into the OCRA coaching pathway.

It establishes the core standards expected of people beginning their coaching journey and introduces areas including:

- The role and responsibilities of a coach
- Athlete welfare
- Safeguarding
- Disability awareness and inclusion
- Health and safety
- Ethical coaching
- Creating a safe and respectful training environment

Foundational requirements may also include appropriate safeguarding training, first aid and vetting where applicable.

OCR Coach — Participation

Participation prepares coaches to deliver safe, structured and enjoyable OCR sessions for beginners and recreational athletes.

It focuses on areas including:

- Fundamental obstacle movement
- Introductory obstacle technique
- Running and physical preparation
- Strength and fitness for OCR
- Safe equipment use
- Athlete readiness
- Session planning
- Demonstration and feedback
- Delivering group training sessions

This level is particularly relevant to coaches working within clubs, gyms, community programmes and participation-focused training environments.

OCR Coach — Performance

Performance develops coaches working with athletes who have competitive goals.

It introduces more advanced areas including:

- Performance-focused obstacle technique
- Running development
- Strength and conditioning
- Athlete performance analysis
- Programme planning
- Periodisation
- Competition preparation
- Recovery
- Nutrition awareness
- Athlete mindset
- Adaptive coaching principles

Coaches at this level should be able to develop structured programmes rather than simply deliver individual training sessions.

OCR Coach — National

The National level is intended for experienced coaches working with advanced competitive athletes and national-level performers.

The focus moves towards longer-term athlete development, including:

- Advanced performance planning
- Seasonal and annual programming
- Training load management
- Competition planning
- Performance review
- Athlete progression
- National Championship preparation
- Supporting athletes progressing towards international competition

International Coaching

The higher levels of the pathway recognise coaches working within international and high-performance environments.

These are:

- OCR Coach — International
- OCR Coach — Senior International
- OCR Coach — Master Coach Trainer

Progression at these levels requires substantial coaching experience and increased responsibility.

At the highest level, coaches may also contribute to:

- Coach education
- Coach mentoring
- Assessment
- National coaching development
- Maintaining coaching standards
- Developing the future coaching pathway

Adaptive and Inclusive Coaching

Obstacle sport should provide meaningful opportunities for athletes with different abilities and requirements.

Inclusive and adaptive coaching principles are included throughout the OCRA coaching pathway.

OCRA also intends to support specialist development for coaches working specifically with adaptive and para-athletes.

Coach Development

Becoming qualified should not mark the end of a coach's education.

Coaches should continue developing through a combination of:

- Practical coaching experience
- Further education
- Workshops
- Mentoring
- Rule and technical updates
- Safeguarding updates
- Reflection and performance review
- Continuing Professional Development

As obstacle sport develops, coaching practice must develop with it.

Recognition of Previous Experience

OCRA recognises that people entering obstacle sport coaching may already have significant experience or qualifications from other sports and professional backgrounds.

Relevant experience may include areas such as:

- Athletics
- Strength and conditioning
- Personal training
- Climbing
- Gymnastics
- Ninja sport
- Parkour
- Functional fitness
- Endurance sport
- Other relevant coaching disciplines

Where appropriate, OCRA may consider previous education and practical experience through a Recognition of Prior Learning process.

This does not remove the need to meet the standards specific to obstacle sport, safeguarding and athlete welfare.

Who Is the Coaching Pathway For?

The pathway is suitable for people who want to:

- Begin coaching OCR
- Coach within an OCR club
- Deliver beginner and recreational sessions
- Work with competitive athletes
- Develop youth athletes
- Support adaptive athletes
- Coach national-level competitors
- Work towards international coaching
- Become involved in coach education

Interested in Becoming an OCR Coach?

You do not need to already be an elite athlete to become a good coach.

Coaching requires the ability to understand athletes, communicate effectively, plan appropriately, create safe environments and help people develop over time.

